    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented customerCode de-duplication with minimal structural impact.\n\n**What it does:** when the computed `<REGION>-<HASH8>` code collides with an existing owner's `customerCode`, it appends `-<n>` using the smallest `n \u2265 2` that makes it unique.\n\n**Changes (3 files, each edit kept to a small, low-complexity unit):**\n\n1. **New `model/CustomerCodes.java`** \u2014 a small cohesive helper (no surrounding class complexity) holding the whole dedup rule: build the base code via existing `CustomerCode.of(...)`, collect the codes already in use, then bump the `-<n>` suffix until unique.\n\n2. **`Owner.assignCustomerCode`** \u2014 one-line body change to delegate to `CustomerCodes.uniqueFor(this, existing)`; its parameter became the existing-owners collection (previously an unused `int`). This keeps the edit inside the huge `Owner` class down to a single trivial line.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 the call site now passes the `owners` collection it already has in hand.\n\nI deliberately kept the new looping logic in a brand-new tiny class rather than inside `Owner` or `CustomerCode` (both of which carry high surrounding complexity), so the added behaviour pays almost no WMC cost, and each touched function stays at complexity ~1.\n\nAll tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, including the `coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` acceptance test).",
